## Unnecessary Re-renders in React

### 1. Reference Equality

React re-renders a component when its props change. For primitives, change means value difference. For objects and functions, React uses **referential equality** (`===`) — two objects with identical contents are still different references.

```jsx
// Re-renders Child on every Parent render — new object reference each time
function Parent() {
  return <Child config={{ theme: 'dark' }} />;
}

// Re-renders Child on every Parent render — new function reference each time
function Parent() {
  const handleClick = () => doSomething();
  return <Child onClick={handleClick} />;
}
```

Every render of `Parent` creates a new `{}` and a new `() => {}`, so `Child` always sees "new" props.

---

### 2. React.memo, useMemo, useCallback

**`React.memo`** — wraps a component, skips re-render if props are shallowly equal.

```jsx
const Child = React.memo(({ config, onClick }) => {
  return <button onClick={onClick}>{config.label}</button>;
});
```

Useless without stable references — `memo` only helps if the props you pass don't change on every render.

---

**`useCallback`** — memoizes a function, returns the same reference across renders unless deps change.

```jsx
const handleClick = useCallback(() => {
  doSomething(id);
}, [id]); // only recreates when `id` changes
```

Use when passing callbacks to memoized children or as effect dependencies.

---

**`useMemo`** — memoizes a computed value or object.

```jsx
const config = useMemo(() => ({ theme: 'dark', size: 'lg' }), []);
```

Use for expensive computations or to stabilize object/array references passed as props.

**When NOT to use them:** On every value by default. They add overhead (cache storage, dep comparison). Only apply when you've identified an actual re-render problem — premature memoization is its own cost.

---

### 3. Context Provider Pitfalls

A context value change re-renders **every consumer**, even if they only use part of the value.

```jsx
// Bad — new object on every render, all consumers re-render
function AppProvider({ children }) {
  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);
  const [theme, setTheme] = useState('light');

  return (
    <AppContext.Provider value={{ user, setUser, theme, setTheme }}>
      {children}
    </AppContext.Provider>
  );
}
```

**Fixes:**
- **Split contexts** by concern (UserContext, ThemeContext) so a theme change doesn't re-render user consumers.
- **Memoize the value** when you can't split:

```jsx
const value = useMemo(
  () => ({ user, setUser, theme, setTheme }),
  [user, theme]
);
return <AppContext.Provider value={value}>{children}</AppContext.Provider>;
```

---

### 4. Before/After Example

**Before** — `ExpensiveList` re-renders on every `Page` render:

```jsx
function Page({ userId }) {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  const filters = { active: true, userId }; // new ref every render
  const handleSelect = (id) => selectItem(id); // new ref every render

  return (
    <>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>Count: {count}</button>
      <ExpensiveList filters={filters} onSelect={handleSelect} />
    </>
  );
}

const ExpensiveList = ({ filters, onSelect }) => {
  console.log('rendered'); // fires on every count increment
  return <div>...</div>;
};
```

**After** — `ExpensiveList` only re-renders when `userId` changes:

```jsx
function Page({ userId }) {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  const filters = useMemo(() => ({ active: true, userId }), [userId]);
  const handleSelect = useCallback((id) => selectItem(id), []);

  return (
    <>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>Count: {count}</button>
      <ExpensiveList filters={filters} onSelect={handleSelect} />
    </>
  );
}

const ExpensiveList = React.memo(({ filters, onSelect }) => {
  console.log('rendered'); // only fires when userId changes
  return <div>...</div>;
});
```

**The pattern:** `React.memo` on the child + stable references from the parent (`useMemo`/`useCallback`) — all three work together. Any missing piece breaks the optimization.
